# Probe 302n, item a (a baseline on the 301 tuple; CODEX-OPUS-AUTHOR-NAMED-MERGE-ASSIGN-DISCUSS-20261107-01): the
# controls of an ordinary call of tst (int: c 3; int: a) with no callable a visible -- by position tst(4, 5), wanted 45,
# and by the present named form tst(a: 5), wanted 35 (c its written default 3).  The author's name test is a word of the
# language (the receiver test:), which a program cannot bind; tst keeps the shape.  Entry 7.  An exit below 100 is the
# positional call's value (91..93 its refusal: call, implements, convert); 100 + v is the named call's value v (191..193
# its refusal).  Nothing here is a norm: the record states what the 301 tuple does.
fn: tst (int: c 3; int: a) int
    return: c * 10 + a
end: tst
fn: positional () int
    ---
        int: got tst(4, 5)
        return: got
        catch: call ()
            return: 91
        catch: implements ()
            return: 92
        catch: convert ()
            return: 93
    ---
end: positional
fn: named () int
    ---
        int: got tst(a: 5)
        return: got
        catch: call ()
            return: 91
        catch: implements ()
            return: 92
        catch: convert ()
            return: 93
    ---
end: named
fn: run () int
    int: p positional()
    if: p != 45
        return: p
    ---
    int: q named()
    if: q != 35
        return: q + 100
    ---
    return: 7
end: run
int: code run()
sendMessage: exit(exit_code: code; stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
